-- title:
--   Cusps of X_H(N) unramified over X₀(N)
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and $H$ a subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z}/N)^\times$, and write $\Gamma_H(N)$ for the subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ obtained as the image, under the inclusion of $\Gamma_0(N)$, of the set of matrices in $\Gamma_0(N)$ whose lower-right entry reduces mod $N$ into $H$. Assume that for every $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ and every $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, if $\gamma T^k \gamma^{-1}$ lies in $\Gamma_0(N)$ then it already lies in $\Gamma_H(N)$, $T$ being the standard unipotent generator. Inside $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}((q))$ consider the intermediate fields $K$ and $K_0$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ which are assumed equal to the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-subfields generated by the coefficientwise images of the $q$-expansion function fields $F(\Gamma_H(N))$ and $F(\Gamma_0(N))$ respectively; here $F(\Gamma)$ is the subfield of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ generated by the ratios of integral $q$-expansions of pairs of modular forms of equal weight for $\Gamma$. Assume $K_0 \le K$, and let $j \in K$ be an element whose underlying Laurent series is the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion $q^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{jNumQ}(q)$ of the modular invariant. Let $P$ be a place of $K$ over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, that is, a proper valuation subring of $K$ containing $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and a principal ideal ring, and suppose $\operatorname{ord}_P(j) < 0$. Then the ramification index of $P$ along the inclusion $K_0 \hookrightarrow K$, namely the least $n > 0$ for which some nonzero $f \in K_0$ has $\operatorname{ord}_P(f) = n$, equals $1$.
--
--   This is the statement that $X_H(N) \to X_0(N)$ is unramified at each cusp, in the $q$-expansion function-field model over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, under the hypothesis that every $\Gamma_0(N)$-stabiliser of a cusp already lies in $\Gamma_H(N)$ (equality of cusp widths); places with $\operatorname{ord}_P(j) < 0$ are exactly the cusps. It feeds the computations of ramification indices along the inclusions attached to full-level covers used in the level-lowering arguments.
